<!--Post: 13570--><!--Time: 1115717468-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13570"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 10, 2005 - 2:31 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It seems highly unlikely Dennis Rader will plead guilty in a trial. A guilty plea would seem probable in a trial when the punishment is the death penalty. Rader believes he possesses the ingenuity and acquired the legal expertise to avoid a life sentence without the possibility of parole.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13570-->
<!--Post: 13578--><!--Time: 1115738626-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13578"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 10, 2005 - 8:23 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy - I have a question for you: Do you ever discuss with your convicts Dennis Rader in your correspondence with them? If so, what do they think about him? If not, I would lke to know what they think about him.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13578-->
<!--Post: 13588--><!--Time: 1115764009-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13588"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 10, 2005 - 3:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky, <BR> <BR>I mentioned the BTK Strangler to a few serial murderers before the end of February 2005. They tend to distance themselves from other serial murderers. A few years ago a serial murderer acknowledged some interest in the BTK Strangler investigation. He thought the BTK Strangler was most probably dead. Other serial murderers suggest they are not sadistic psychopaths who enjoy murder. They believe the BTK Strangler and other prominent serial murderers are more manipulative and dominant than them. <BR> <BR>The subject of Dennis Rader has not emerged in discussions with any serial murderer since his arrest in late February. The topic may emerge in the near future. Serial murderers prefer to focus on their life, problems, relationships and blaming a particular police department, poor forensic science and poor criminal investigation for their predicament. A sadistic psychopath is primarily concerned with satisfying their perverse and violent desires. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13588-->
<!--Post: 13591--><!--Time: 1115768548-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13591"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Beck<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Beck<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 10, 2005 - 4:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Phoenix, You&#39;re right, insanity is the best defense for him, because it&#39;s his only, it doesn&#39;t make him actually insane. I wish I had more of your legal knowlege, and I want to thank you for offering your insight in regards this case. <BR> <BR>It just seems so cut and dry. A man who is a compiance officer. He has to know wrongs in that line of work, it&#39;s part of the job. A man who is not only religous, but a prominent member of their church. God-fearing christians resist sin, I&#39;m sure they know right from wrong. BTK hid behind a typewritter to boast about what he was doing. If he didn&#39;t think about right and wrong, he would have done his boasting face to face with people. The jerk submitted an apology in his first letter for allowing this to happen to society. He&#39;s guilty, let&#39;s get to it, and move on, for the victim&#39;s sake.  <BR> <BR>I think that we have the best justice system in the world, but somtimes I think that we over-compensate trying to be fair in trials, so much so that the most guilty end up walking free. That scares me!!!  <BR> <BR>&#42;jumped off soapbox&#42;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13591-->
<!--Post: 13594--><!--Time: 1115771738-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13594"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 10, 2005 - 5:35 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->What reasoning do the serial murderers give to feel compelled to murder over and over again or do they even elaborate on what compels them to do so? <BR> <BR>BTW Ivy - your so gracious to share this with us! Thank you! <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13594-->
<!--Post: 13598--><!--Time: 1115774656-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13598"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 10, 2005 - 6:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The principle of the typical serial murderer is a myth. One serial murderer may be motivated by his inability to have romantic relationships with women and another may hate his mother. A few have expressed their inability to develop caring and loving relationships with women as the source for their compulsion to murder. In reality, some are not concerned with developing romantic relationships with women. They consider the victim a vessel to be sexually abused and or physically abused before murdering them. The act of murder provides the perpetrator with sexual gratification. Some enjoy watching the victim&#39;s face express fear and terror, others remain at the scene to view the affects of death and others leave after stabbing or firing the weapon.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13598-->
<!--Post: 13600--><!--Time: 1115777412-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13600"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 10, 2005 - 7:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy - Their inability to develop caring and loving realationships with women. You know, there are many women who are incapable of developing a caring and loving relationship with a man, but they are not serial killers! This is all very interesting to me. Why do they always feel compelled to blame women? Does testosterone really inflame a males frustration with women to this level? <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13600-->
<!--Post: 13608--><!--Time: 1115782642-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13608"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 10, 2005 - 8:37 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky, <BR> <BR>There are many men and women who are not able to develop relationships with the opposite gender and most of them do not commit serial murder. Neuroscience currently cannot provide an adequate explanation for some men and women becoming serial murderers. Hormones are part of the equation, but the extent cannot be determined at the moment. Further research into neuroscience is required to understand the human psyche and the factors motivating some people to commit serial murder. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13608-->
<!--Post: 13609--><!--Time: 1115787723-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13609"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 10, 2005 - 10:02 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky: <BR> <BR>The sad, horrid, fascinating reality on repeat violent offenders is that they are almost always male. <BR> <BR>The accepted profile of serial killers has been a white male, past puberty &#40;18-20 but not yet into the mid 40&#39;s male menopause. <BR> <BR>Most do their killing between the ages of 18-20 and 48-50. <BR> <BR>Most are sexual psychopaths.  [Although in light of the body of emerging research, I wonder if the cases are driving the diagnosis and then definition of insanity. I worry that the criminals &#40;through their crimes&#41; are defining the Insanity diagnosis to the point that it lacks any predictive value] <BR>. <BR>Think of their demands that they be &#34;crowned&#34; BTK, Son of Sam&#39;, Jack The Ripper,&#39; Wane Gacey. <BR> <BR>Are we seeing an established Dissociative personality helping a new alter find a name? <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13609-->
<!--Post: 13620--><!--Time: 1115821297-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13620"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, May 11, 2005 - 7:21 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks you guys! Neuroscience seems to be focused on controlling or finding answers to the diseases at hand that afflict the non-criminal members of society. Yet, our prisons continue to be over populated with souls that very well may carry the answers to a lot of the mental disease non-criminals have. A Clockwork Orange was an ill attempt to address the criminal diseased mind. So what keeps the non-criminals from crossing the threshold into forming the mens rea of repeated killings? Therapy, drugs, more therapy, more drugs? I guess half the battle here is recognizing as an individual you may have a mental disease. If I were to develop repeated thoughts of strangling the life out of somebody, I would think I would have enough presence of mind to recogize that there was a serious problem upstairs and seek help. Why didn&#39;t BTK do so? Hatered towards women, the gratification, a superior driving force of getting even etc... <BR> <BR>Phoenix - IMO- the cases are driving the standard for diagnosis and definition of insanity. Your right, and it does affect the predictive value and the DID defense very well may set a new precedent for Kansas. I&#39;m sure they&#39;ll blame that on women too!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13620-->
</table></td></tr></table>
